    ":question/id": "f95fdc58-1eb7-5580-ab34-aae2969c5c74",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-107420",
    ":question/problem": "Calculate ${f}^{'}(x)$ if $f(x)={x}^{3}-{e}^{1-3x}.$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y={e}^{1-3x}$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=1-3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{1-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the sum rule of differentiation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-{e}^{1-3x}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-(-3{e}^{1-3x}) \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+{e}^{1-3x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
  {
