    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-48007",
    ":question/problem": "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=-2{e}^{4x-1}+3.$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=-2{e}^{u}+3,\\,u=4x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-2{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{u}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-8{e}^{4x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
