    ":question/problem": "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=-3{e}^{5-x}$ at the point where $x=5-ln⁡(2).$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=-3{e}^{u},\\,u=5-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-3{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{u}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =3{e}^{u} \\\\  & =3{e}^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=5-ln⁡(2)$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=5-ln⁡(2)} & =3{e}^{5-(5-ln⁡(2))} \\\\  & =3{e}^{ln⁡(2)} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  }
